# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/005_inlining_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 005_inlining_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Small static function results unchanged whether inlined or called, at -O0, -O1 and -O2
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow: the largest computed value
is factorial(7) at 5040, the recursion is bounded at depth 7 so every intermediate product
stays far inside int range, accumulate(7) totals 28, and c * 3 is 33. Every function returns
a value on every path: clamp has three returns and factorial has two. In accumulate, total
and i are initialized before use, so there is no read of uninitialized storage. No shift
operator, no division, no pointer arithmetic, no aliasing violation, and no object modified
twice between sequence points. No dependence on padding bytes or on relative addresses.
The nested_calls line passes three function calls as arguments to a fourth, and their
relative evaluation order is unspecified; however all six static functions are pure, read no
volatile object and modify nothing outside their own frame, so there are zero side-effecting
argument expressions and the unspecified order cannot affect the result.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of a
given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int is used,
which is 32 bits on all four targets, and long is never used because sizeof(long) is 4 on
i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Plain char is not used, so the measured plain-char
signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64) cannot
be observed. No address, pointer value, function pointer, sizeof or _Alignof result is
printed. No floating point is used. Arguments are sourced from volatile storage so the calls
are genuinely materialized at -O0, which exercises each target's calling convention: System V
AMD64 on x86-64, cdecl with all arguments on the stack on i686, AAPCS64 on AArch64 and LP64D
on RISC-V 64. Whether the reference compiler inlines those calls at -O1 or -O2 while bcc
emits real calls at every level is a code-generation difference with no observable effect,
so the printed values are identical on every target at every level.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
add3=24
scale=36
clamp_low=10
clamp_mid=15
clamp_high=20
accumulate=28
factorial=5040
combine=21
nested_calls=70
END
